 - [x] 3.5 Add account-status and session invalidation: login rejects disabled accounts; the JWT carries the user's `token_version` (`ver`); the access resolver rejects a disabled account or version mismatch on every request; disable, re-enable and password reset bump the version; self-registration assigns the seeded `student` role. Verify disabled login returns 401, an old token is rejected after disable and after reset, a re-enabled account still needs a new login, and a newly registered user has the student role.
 
 ## 4. SQL rewriting and CTE fix
 
 - [x] 4.1 Fix CTE scoping in `DataScopeRewriter`: a non-recursive CTE's own name is not visible inside its body, only previously defined CTEs are; reject CTE names that collide with protected tables; reject recursive CTEs in this phase. Verify tests for same-name masking, schema-qualified body, nested CTE, legal CTE, and recursive rejection.
 - [x] 4.2 Harden the row-predicate builder: quote identifiers, escape string values consistently with MySQL, and reject unknown/unsafe identifiers. Verify tests for quote/backslash handling and identifier rejection.
 - [x] 4.3 Add a regression test that a restricted user's `WITH ... AS (SELECT * FROM protected_table)` query is rejected or filtered and returns no out-of-scope rows.
 
 ## 5. Admin API
 
-- [ ] 5.1 Add role CRUD and hierarchy endpoints under `/api/admin/access-control/roles`, including built-in protection, code immutability, and cycle rejection. Verify 200/403/409 cases and audit rows.
+- [x] 5.1 Add role CRUD and hierarchy endpoints under `/api/admin/access-control/roles`, including built-in protection, code immutability, and cycle rejection. Verify 200/403/409 cases and audit rows.
 - [ ] 5.2 Add permission catalog and role-permission matrix endpoints. Verify assigning/revoking a permission changes the next request's effective permission.
 - [ ] 5.3 Add data-scope endpoints with server-side validation against datasource metadata and the principal-attribute catalog. Verify invalid table/column/attribute and raw-SQL input are rejected with 422.
 - [ ] 5.4 Add user management endpoints under `/api/admin/access-control/users`: search/paginate (`user.read`), create with an initial password, edit the display name, disable/enable, reset password, and assign/remove roles (`user.manage`). Password fields are write-only and never echoed back. Verify create/duplicate-username/disable/enable/reset/role-assignment cases, that responses contain no password material, and that every mutation is audited.
 - [ ] 5.5 Migrate the datasource-access matrix to role records and expose it through the access-control API. Verify the existing grant/revoke behavior and the next-request effect.
 - [ ] 5.6 Add audit read endpoints and write audit rows for every access-control mutation, including denied escalation attempts and user lifecycle events without password material. Verify audit rows contain actor, before/after, result, and that `audit.read` is required.
 - [ ] 5.7 Implement anti-escalation and lockout protection: only `access.manage` manages roles/permissions/data scopes, `user.manage` manages users, no self-grant of permissions the actor lacks (including roles assigned during user creation), the last `access.manage` holder cannot be removed, disabled, or have the admin role revoked, and the built-in admin cannot be deleted/disabled. Verify each rejection with a test.
 
 ## 6. Frontend access-control area
 
 - [ ] 6.1 Add TypeScript types and API client functions for roles, permissions, data scopes, users, datasource grants, and audit. Verify the client compiles and each function maps to the backend route.
